The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
BUG_ON(len + offset > PAGE_SIZE << compound_order(page));
BUG_ON(offset >= PAGE_SIZE);
```
Reason:
These assertions incorrectly trigger when handling fragmented SKB data that crosses a page boundary, particularly for tail pages within a compound page. The first assertion fails because it checks if the data length plus the offset exceeds the size of the compound page, which can lead to false positives even when the data is validly contained within the compound page. The second assertion checks if the offset exceeds the page size, which can also be valid in the context of tail pages.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
// Removed the following lines
// BUG_ON(len + offset > PAGE_SIZE << compound_order(page));
// BUG_ON(offset >= PAGE_SIZE);
```
Reason:
By removing these assertions, the code no longer erroneously triggers bugs when handling valid fragmented SKB data that crosses page boundaries. The existing logic already correctly iterates through the pages, making these assertions unnecessary and preventing false positives in scenarios involving tail pages within compound pages.

